'The coast is ours': the country that does not border the coast of Bolivia hopes the court will re-announce the road to the Pacific
The sailors patrol a naval headquarters equipped in La Paz.
The public buildings are flying blue flags.
Naval bases from Titicaca Lake to the Amazon are covered with slogans: “The sea is our right.
“The sea is our mission.”
Every nook and cranny of Bolivia, the memory of a coast lost to the hands of Chile in the bloody resource conflict in the 19th century is still alive – as well as the desire to be sailing to the Pacific Ocean once again.
Those hopes were probably the worst of decades, as Bolivia awaited the international justice court's ruling on October 1 after the five-year deliberation.
Roberto Calzadilla, a Bolivian diplomat: "Bolivia is motivating, a spirit of solidarity and calm, and, of course, is expected in a positive view of the results."
Many Bolivians will follow ICJ’s ruling on major screens across the country, hoping that the court in The Hague will support Bolivia’s claims, that – after decades of tense exchanges – Chile is obligated to Negotiate to give Bolivia a passageway to the sea of sovereignty.
Evo Morales, the persuasive-navigating original President of Bolivia who faces a fierce debate for re-election next year - there are also many cards on Monday's ruling.
At the end of August, he resolutely said: “We are about to return to the Pacific Ocean.”
But some analysts say the court is incapacited to decide to support Bolivia - and this is less likely to change.
The U.N. with headquarters in the Netherlands does not have the right to return the territory to Chile and has stipulated that it will not determine the outcome of potential exchanges.
The ICJ's ruling comes after just six months when the final debates were made that the "uncomplicated situation," said Paz Zárate, an expert on international law of Chile.
And far beyond Bolivia’s litigation, the past four years can be turned back.
Zárate said: "The problem of access to the sea has been attacked by the current Bolivian government."
She added that Morales' belligerent claims have dashed the rest of Chile's goodwill.
At this time, Bolivia and Chile will continue to negotiate with each other, but it will be extremely difficult to organize discussions after this.
The two countries have not swapped ambassadors since 1962.
Eduardo Rostríguez Veltzé, a representative of Bolivia in The Hague, has refuted the idea that the court's decision-making was unusually fast.
Monday will give Bolivia “extremely an extraordinary opportunity to open up a new epoch with a relationship with Chile” and an opportunity to end 139 years of disappropriation of mutual interests,” he said.
Calzadilla has also denied that Morales - remains one of the most well-renowned presidents in Latin America - is using maritime affairs as a political life buoyant.
“Bolivia will never relinquiroze access to the Pacific.”
Judgment is an opportunity to see that we need to cross the past.
