John Edward: Foreign language skills are essential for global citizens
Private schools in Scotland still maintain their excellent academic performance, the event continues in 2018 with a series of commendable academic results, reinforced by both individuals and groups in terms of sports, arts, music and community activities.
With more than 30,000 puppies all over Scotland, these schools, sponsored by the Scottish Private School Board (SCIS), are trying to bring the best quality of service to students and parents.
Independent schools aim to equip themselves with deep and broad knowledge, as well as the career orientation and position of students as global citizens.
As a sub-disciplinary of education that can design and run school-specific curriculum, we see that modern foreign languages continue to be a popular and chosen topic in schools.
Nelson Mandela once said: “If you talk to someone in the language he understands, the things they say will get into his mind.
“If you talk to him in his own language, the things you’re going to get into his feelings.”
It’s a persuasive message that we don’t just rely on English when it comes to establishing relationships and trust with people from other countries.
From the recent exams of this year, we can see that foreign languages are at the forefront of the rankings with the best scores in private schools.
A total of 68 per cent of foreign language students scored higher than grade A.
The data collected from 74 SCIS member schools found that 72 per cent of pupils scored more than A-scratches in Chinese, in addition to 72 per cent learning German, 69 per cent studying French and 63 per cent studying Spanish also scored A.
This shows that private schools in Scotland are in the direction of treating foreign languages as essential skills that children and adolescents won’t be unmatched in the future.
Elegant foreign language subjects are taken into account as STEM (disciplines, technology, engineering, and mathematics) in study programs in private schools as well as in other places.
A survey conducted conducted by the British Proficiency and Proficiency Council in 2014 found that the reasons employers had difficulty in finding people were due to a shortage of foreign language skills.
Therefore, foreign language skills are becoming more and more practical to equip young people to step into professional practice in the future.
With more and more career prospects requiring a foreign language, these skills become practical in an ever-than-blown world.
No matter how people choose to work, if they learn a second foreign language, they will gain a real advantage in the future when there is such a lifelong skill.
Being able to Communiculate directly in the language of the natives themselves, a multi-speaker will obviously lead every challenge.
According to a survey by YouGov on 4,000 adults in the United Kingdom in 2013, 75 per cent of them were unable to practice a simple passage in another foreign language, and only French was the only foreign language with a number of units, 15 per cent.
That’s the reason why investing in foreign language teaching is essential for today’s children.
Holding multilingual hands, especially the languages of the developing economies, will equip children with the potential to be brighter in finding a real job.
In Scotland, each school teaches different foreign languages.
Some schools will focus on more modern foreign languages, while others will teach foreign languages that are considered critical to the UK in their vision to 2020, such as Chinese or Japanese.
No matter what children are interested, there are a variety of foreign languages to choose within private schools, along with a team of teachers who have expertise in the profession.
Private schools in Scotland are focused on providing an academic environment that prepares and equips children with essential skills for success, no matter what the future lurks.
Undeniable that, in an international business environment, foreign languages will continue to be an extremely important factor in the country’s future, which should be replicated in education.
In fact, state-of-the-line foreign languages should be considered “international communication skills.”
Private schools will continue to offer this opportunity for Scottish youth: diversity and excommunicence.
There can be no other option.
John Edward is the Director of the Scottish Private School Board.
