North Korea Says 'Non-life' Separation Unrivaled Without Confidence
The North's foreign minister told the U.N. on Saturday, asserting that the sanctions are still being kept deepening its disbelief towards the U.S. and in this situation, there is no way that it unilaterally renounces nuclear weapons.
Ri Yong Ho spoke during an annual U.N. General Assembly session, said North Korea has implemented "many goodwill measures" over the past year, such as discontinuing nuclear and missile tests, dismantling nuclear test bases and a commitment not to proliferate nuclear weapons and nuclear technology.
“But we don’t see a similar move from the U.S.,” he said.
“If we couldn’t trust the U.S., we wouldn’t be able to confidently guarantee national security, and in such a situation, there’s no way we’d permanently disarm ourselves.”
Although Ri still repeated North Korea's familiar complaint about Washington's refusal to disarmament of nuclear weapons "phaseally" and North Korea will receive the corresponding benefit of taking step-by-step, his statement has a notable point of disappropriability of unilateral dismantling of nuclear weapons as Pyongyang previously refused.
Ri referred to a joint statement issued by Kim Jong Un and Donald Trump at the first summit between an incumbent U.S. president and a North Korean leader in Singapore on June 12. In this statement, Kim pledged his efforts toward "destroying nuclear weapons on the Korean Peninsula," while Trump promised to ensure North Korea's security.
“Korean Korea is still trying to find a way to formally end the Korean War in 1950-53, but the U.S. insists that Pyongyang had to give up its nuclear weapons first.
Washington has also protested its calls for cessary international sanctions against North Korea.
“The U.S. insists on “destroying nuclear weapons in advance” and increasing the pressure with sanctions to achieve its goals in a coercive way, even protesting “declaring an cessation of the end of the war,” Ri said.
“The notion that sanctions can make us kneel succumb is only the wild dreams of those who don’t know anything about us.
“But the problem here is that these ongoing sanctions continue to dissue our trust.”
Ri didn’t mention his plans to hold a second summit between President Kim and President Trump as the U.S. leader presented to the United Nations earlier this week.
Instead, the foreign minister mentioned three meetings between President Kim and South Korean President Moon Jae-in over the past five months and added: “If the negotiating side of nuclear disarmament is South Korea, not the U.S., the denuclearization of the Korean Peninsula will not come at the current standoff.”
However, Ri’s tone was very different from last year, when he told the General Assembly that North Korea’s launch of missiles into the U.S. Confederate was unavoidable in a suicide mission after “The Evil President” Trump called Kim “The Rocket Man”.
Last year at the U.N., Trump threatened to "detegate" North Korea last year, praising Kim for bravely taking dissolution, but Trump also stressed that there is a lot of work to be done and that sanctions must remain the same until North Korea disarming its nuclear weapons.
On Wednesday, Trump said he didn’t set a deadline: “It doesn’t matter for two years, three-years or just five months.”
China and Russia argue that the U.N. Security Council should reward Pyongyang for its efforts.
U.S. Secretary of US Secretary of State Mike Pompeo on Thursday told the U.N. Security Security Council: “Balanthropic sanctions must continue to be carried out vigorously and uncommended until we are able to corroborate complete denuclearization results, ultimately, with evidence of verification.”
The Security Council unanimous unanimously stepped up sanctions against North Korea since 2006 aimed at deterring funding Pyongyang's ballistic and nuclear missile programs.
Secretary of State Pompeo held Foreign Minister Ri on the sidelines of official U.S. General Assembly meetings and said he would visit Pyongyang again next month to prepare for the second summit.
Pompeo has visited North Korea three times this year, but the results of the final visit are not very positive.
He left Pyongyang after the visit in July and said the talks had made certain progress, but just a few hours later, the North Korean accused the foreign minister had made "black society-like demands."
"Korea pledged to take part in a meeting with South Korean President Moon Jae-In this month aimed at disassembling a missile test base and a nuclear-combination if the U.S. has a "similar move."
He said Kim directed that "similar move he's looking for is a security guarantee that Trump has committed during talks in Singapore and proceeded to normalize relations with Washington.
Harvard students take a course on adequate rest.
Efforts to combat machine-criminatory learning culture, with 'Night owl students' drinking water-changing coffee, a new course at this year's Harvard University has encouraged all university students to sleep more.
Students who study in the number one university in this world often do not know the basic knowledge of how to take care of themselves.
Charles Czeisler, a professor of sleep medicine at the Harvard Medical School, is also an expert at the Women’s and Brigham Hospital who designed the course, and he himself believes that this is the first course in this category in the United States.
Inspiration started this course came after he took part in a talk about the effects of sleep depriviction on the learning process.
“At the end of the conversation, a female student came to see me and said: ‘Why did you know this in the last year?’
She said that no one told her about the importance of sleep, which made me really surprised,” he told The Telegraph.
The course was introduced for the first time this this year, explaining to students the importance of good sleep habits that improve their learning and sports results, while improving their overall health.
Paul Barreira, a professor of psychaeology at the Harvard Medical University and CEO of medical services at the university, said the school decided to recommend the course after discovering students with severe sleep deprivation during the week.
The one-hour-breathing course consists of a wide range of interactive tasks.
In a part there is a dormitory image, in which students can click on coffee cups, curtains, exercise machines and books to learn about the effects of caffeine, light and sleep deprivation that affect the results of sports training, as well as the importance of forming the habit of going to bed on time.
In a different part, participants who are likely to find out what long-term sleep deprivation may have an increased risk of heart attacks, strokes, depression, and cancer.
Then the university campus map, with interactive symbols, encourages participants to think about their day-to-day habits.
"We know that this course can't immediately change the behavior of students.
“But we believe they have the right to know – just as you have the right to affect your health when it comes to choosing to smoke,” Czeisler added.
“Food-promoted “Night owl students” still exists, while the modern technologies and increasing pressure on students lead to sleep deprivation is an ever-increasing problem.
Ensuring adequate sleep, good sleep quality should become the “secret weapon” of students to fight stress, exhaustion and anxiety – even to avoid weight gain, because sleep deprivation causes the brain to starvate, leading us to constantly starvation.
Raymond So, a 19-year-old student in California who was studying biology, chemistry and physics, helped Czeissler to design the course, while also taking part in one of last year’s classes in his first year at Harvard.
He said the course helped him “open his eyes” and inspiring him to make an effort to organize a course throughout the school.
He hoped that the next step would be to ask all post-a-educated students to participate in a similar study program before starting to compete in the main course.
Czeisler advises students to consider when setting a bed-to-e-to-wake, wake-wake, while also being aware of the harmful effects of ‘blue light’ coming out of an electronic screen and an LED, which can disrupt your circadian arrhythmia, resulting in difficulty sleeping.
