The Introducator of the Geiger Counter
Excluding a page from "Saturday Morning": On September 30/1882, 136 years ago and KEPT COLLABORATATURATATABILIZER Johannes Wilhelm "Hans" Geiger was born in Germany.
Geiger has developed a method of detecting and measuring radiation, inventing the foundation for the device called the Geiger Counter.
Since then, the Geiger Counter has not only become the pillar of science, but is also the mainstay of popular culture. The following example is a dialogue in the film "Bells of Coronado", which premiered in 1950, with the participation of scientists and cowboy king Roy Rogers and Dale Evans:
The man: "What is this?"
Rogers: "This is the Geiger Counter, which is used to locate radioactive minerals, such as uranium.
“When you put these earphones on, you can actually hear the effects of the atoms caused by the radiation in the minerals.”
Evans: “That’s right, there’s a noise here!”
"Hans" Geiger died in 1945, just a few days after his 63rd birthday.
However, the invention that bears his name is still left with time.
