What is the Global Celebrity Festival and does it make any contribution to Poverty Eliminance?
On this Saturday, New York will host the Global Citizens Festivities, an annual music event with the participation of an impressive cast of stars to carry out an equally impressive mission: the cessation of poverty in the world.
In the seventh year of this event, the Global Citizens Festival will witness tens of thousands of people flocking to the Central Park’s Great Lawn Lawn not only to enjoy Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes performances, but also to raise awareness about the true goal of the event to cessulate extreme impoverishment by 2030.
The Global Celebrity Festival, which has been held since 2012, is an extension of the Global Poverty Eliminatory Penalty, an international mobilization team in the hope of ending poverty by increasing the number of people actively struggling against poverty.
In order to receive free entrance tickets for this event (unless you’re willing to pay for a VIP ticket), music night attendees must complete a variety of tasks or “actions” such as volunteering, sending an email to a world leader, making a phone call, or any other meaningful form to help raise awareness about poverty alleviation goals.
But how has this Global Citizens Festival achieved, when there are now only 12 years left to achieve the set goals?
Is the idea of a free one-night ticket reward for people a practical way to persuading people to call for action, or is it just a different form of "keyboard movement" - do people feel they're making a real difference by signing an online petition or submitting a Twitter post?
“Since 2011, the Global Citizens Organization says more than 10 million “actions” from supporters are recognized, promoting a wide range of different targets.
They say that these actions have helped motivate leaders around the world to make commitments and policies equivalent to more than $37 billion set to implement interventions that improve the lives of more than 2.25 billion people by 2030.
In early 2018, the group cites 390 commitments and the announcement comes from the group’s actions, with at least $10 billion disbursed or fundraised.
It is estimated so far that secured funds have had a direct impact of nearly 649 million people around the world.
A number of key pledges include The Power of Nutrition, a partner organization consisting of investors and implementers who pledged to “enable children to the best of their abilities,” promising to provide $25 million to Rwanda to help end malnutrition in the country after receiving more than 4700 Twitter posts from the Global Citizens.
"With support from the British government, donor donors, national governments and global citizens just like you, we can turn social infidelity into a milestone in history," the Health of the Nutrition Program Ambassador, Tracey Ullman shared with the audience in a live performance in London in April 2018.
The group also said that, after more than 5,000 actions urging the United Kingdom to improve nutrition for mothers and children, the government has announced funding for a project called Nutritional Power, ensuring nutritional intervention for 5 million women and children.
Responding to one of the Frequently Asked Questions on the group’s website “What makes you believe we can put an end to extreme impoveriment?”
"It will be a long-distance and difficult road - we will sometimes fail.
But just like the anti-racticing movements of civil rights and the anti-racist movement, we will be successful because together we will become stronger.
Janet Jackson, the Weeknd, Shawn Mendes, Cardi B, Janelle Monáe are a number of artists who will be participating in this year's event in New York, hosted by Deborra-Lee Furness and Hugh Jackman.
