World Wide Web Innovator Plans to Start Building New Internet to Receiving Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, inventor of the World Wide Web, is currently launching a start-up project aimed to compete with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
This tech legend’s latest project is called Inrupt, a building company based on Berners-Lee’s open-source.
Solid allows users to choose where to store the data and who is granted permissions to access any information.
In an exclusive interview with Fast Company, Berners-Lee joked that the intention behind the Inrupt project was "dominating the world."
“Let’s get started right away,” he’s talking about this start-up project.
“It’s now a historic moment.”
The application uses Solid technology, which allows users to create "online personal data trousers" of their own.
This data warehouse may include contact lists, to-do lists, calendars, music libraries, personal and professional instruments.
Similar to Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify, it’s all available on one browser and displayed at the same time.
What’s unique about this online personal data repository is that users have the full right to decide who can access what kind of information.
The company calls it "gives control of data for individuals."
According to the company’s CEO John Bruce, the Inrupt project’s idea is to ensure the company’s resources, processes, and skills to help provide Solid to everyone.
The existing company Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security platform has been acquired by IBM, some developers who have contracted work under the project and a community of volunteer programmers.
Starting this week, technology developers around the world are able to manually create their own decentralized applications using the tools available on the Inrupt website.
Berners-Lee said he and his team didn’t talk to “Facebook and Google about whether it’s completely changed because all of their business models can be upgraded overnight.
“We don’t have to ask permission from them.”
In a Post on Medium on Saturday, Berners-Lee wrote "Inrupt's mission is to provide commercial energy and an ecosystem that helps to protect the integrity and quality of the new website built on the Solid platform."
In 1994, Berners-Lee converted the Internet when he founded the World Wide Web Association at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.
In recent months, Berners-Lee is an influential name in the debate about the neutrality of the internet.
Despite the Inrupt launch, Berners-Lee will continue to serve as the Founder and Director of the World Wide Web Association, the Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
"I am extremely optimistic about the next era of the web," Berners-Lee added.
