The National Trust eavesdropped on the secret lives of bats
A new study is being carried out at a property in the Scottish Plateau to reveal how bats use the natural landscape to hunt for food.
It is hoped that these findings will uncircumcise the behavior of this unconfining mammal and orthopaede for future conservation activities.
The study was carried out by scientists of the National Trust for Scotland, which tracks the journey of popular bats, soprano pipistrelles, brown-eared bat and Daubenton bat at the Inverewe Gardens in Wester Ross.
Special recorders will be placed in important locations around the research site to monitor the activity of these bats throughout the season.
NHS staff and volunteers will also conduct mobile surveys using hand-held detectors.
Audio analyzers will conduct an analysis of all the recordings and determine the sound frequency of bat species and the purpose of the sound of each specific bat.
After that, maps and environmental reports of bats will be set up for detailed paintings according to the natural landscape of their behavior.
Rob Dewar, NTS’ natural conservation advisor, hopes the results will help determine which habitats have the most important role for bats and each species living in that environment.
This information will help to determine the benefits of living environment management activities of organisms, such as pasture cultivation and the best way to conserve forests for bats and other related species.
The bamboo festivities in Scotland and across the United Kingdom have fallen significantly over the last century.
They are threatened by construction and development activities that affect bat bean rigs and loss of habitat.
Wind turbines and light systems, fly-trap paper, a number of methods of chemical treatment for building materials, as well as pet cat's attacks can also pose risks to bats.
Bats aren't completely blind.
However, due to the habit of hunting at night, the ears are more useful than the eyes when stalking and catching bait.
They use an extremely sophisticated resonant positioning technique to identify obstacles in the flight path.
NTS is responsible for taking care of more than 270 historical buildings, 38 important gardens and 76,000 hectares of land in the whole country, while attaching special importance to bats.
The organization has ten well-traintened experts who regularly conduct surveys, examinations and are sometimes involved in animal rescuing.
The organization has even established Scotland's first and only bat sanctuary at the Threave site in Dumfries and Galloway. It is home to eight of Scotland's ten bat species.
The land manager, David Thompson, said the land is their ideal territory.
"Threave is a great living environment for bats," he said.
"We have many old buildings, a lot of trees and good living environment.
But there’s still a lot of mystery about bats, so work here and in other facilities will help us to better understand what bats need to grow further.”
He stressed the importance of having to examine the lives of bats before conservation in the land, because inadvertently destroying their breeding rig can kill up to 400 female and vomit, almost completely exterminating the population.
Bats are protected species and the killing, harassing, deplorifying or deducing their bean truss is illegal.
Elisabeth Ferrell, a Scottish Bat Conservation Foundation employee, has encouraged the community to help.
She said: "We still need to learn more about bats and with many species, we don't know how far apart their populations are."
