Brexit: Britain 'will forever regret' for losing the status of a car manufacturer
U.S. Secretary of the U.S. “will forever regret” if he lost its leading position in post-Brextender car production.
He also added "savvy" when Toyota UK told the BBC that if Britain leaves the European Union with no further agreement, it will stop production at the factory in Burnaston, near Derby.
“We need an agreement,” Clark said.
The Japanese carmaker said delays at the border in the event of a no-a-deal Brexit could lead to a loss of a job.
Burnaston factory - which produced Toyota's Auris and Avensis - produced nearly 150,000 units last year, of which 90% were exported to other countries in the European Union.
"My view is that if Britain completely leaves the European Union at the end of March we will have to dislodge production at this plant," said Marvin Cooke, Toyota's chief executive at Burnaston.
Other carmakers in the U.K. also raised concerns about Britain's exit from the EU without a cross-border trade deal, including Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover.
BMW, for example, said it would close the Mini plant in Oxford in a one-month follow-up Brexit.
The main concern of automobile manufacturers is the supply chain risk in the case of Brexit without another deal.
Toyota's production line operates according to the "just-in-time" (just-time" principle), with the spare parts delivered in the United Kingdom and the European Union after every 37 minutes for vehicles produced on orders.
If Britain leaves the EU without another deal on March 29, there is a possibility that there is a hiatus at the border and the sector fears could lead to delays and shortages of spare parts.
Toyota said the company couldn't keep inventory value for more than a day at the Derbyshire plant, so it would have to stop manufacturing.
Mr Clark said Prime Minister Theresa May' Chequers plans for future ties with the European Union would "corregated correctly to avoid checks at the border."
"We need to have an agreement. We want to have the best deal, not only to guarantee success in the present, but also to make sure we seize this opportunity very well," he said on BBC Radio No. 4.
“Equivalence from Toyota as well as other car manufacturers shows that we need to make sure the supply chain succeeds.”
Toyota hasn’t announced how long it’s going to stop production, but in the long term, it’s warned that additional costs will reduce the factory’s competitiveness and, ultimately, workers will lose their jobs.
Peter Tsouvallaris has been working in Burnaston for 24 years and convened the Unite trade union at the factory, saying his members are increasingly worried: “In my experience, I’ve never lost these jobs.
“We have formulated an accurate and reliable plan for the relationship with the European Union in the future.”
White House Annunciation Announces Meeting Between Trump and Rosenstein Could Resolve
The White House announced on Sunday: President Donald Trump’s high-level meeting with Deputy Attorney General Rod Rosenstein may “take a week off” as the fight over the case of Supreme Court Justice candidate Brett Kavanaugh is continuing.
Rosenstein oversees the work of Special Adversary Robert Mueller, who is investigating an intervening case in the Russian election, links between Trump and Russia’s aides and the possibility that the president interferes with the implementation of justice.
Whether or not Trump’s lieutenant-departmental lieutenant is that Mueller will hardly be able to operate independently, has sparked rumors of Washington for many months.
Earlier this month, the New York Times reported that Rosenstein had discussed wearing earphones to record conversations with Trump and the possibility of ousting the presidency through Judgment No. 25.
Rosenstein refuses to report.
But last Monday, he went to the White House amid speculative rumours that he was about to step down.
Instead, his meeting with President Trump, who was visiting the U.N. General Assembly in New York, was scheduled for Thursday.
Trump said he "doesn't want to" fire Rosenstein but the meeting has been postponed to avoid conflict with the Senate judiciary-committeer hearing between Kavanaugh and Dr. Christine Blasey, one of the women who has accused him of testifying about sexual accusation.
On Friday, President Trump asked the FBI to spend a week investigating the charges against Kavanaugh, while continuing to delay the Senate full vote.
The press clerk of President Trump, Sarah Sanders, appeared on Fox News on Sunday.
When asked about Rosenstein's meeting, she said: "The meeting date hasn''t been fixed, maybe this week, it's possible to see the reason for this postponement is because other issues are taking place in the Supreme Court.
But we’ll soon receive the news and the press will be up-to-date.”
A number of reporters may disappropriately: Sanders hasn’t held a press conference at the White House since Sept.
The presenter Chris Wallace asked her about the reason.
Sanders said there wasn’t a lack of press conferences because she didn’t like “too many stories,” although she once said: “I also agree that journalists are in fact a lot.”
She then offered to increase the direct connection between President Trump and the press.
"The president is currently doing more question-and-answer sessions than any previous president," she added without giving evidence: "We've reviewed the number of press conferences."
Sanders said there would still be news briefings but “if the press had a chance to directly ask the U.S. president, it would be much better than talking to me.
“We’ve tried very hard and you’ve also seen we’ve been doing a lot of work in the last few weeks, this will replace a news conference when you can talk directly to the U.S. president.”
President Trump regularly responds to questions when he leaves the White House or when he participates in opening sessions or during press briefings when officials visit.
Simple press briefings are often very rare.
This week, in New York, the president should have announced the reason but he appeared suddenly in a strange appearance in front of a crowd of reporters.
