Expatriates who buy real estate in the UK will be charged higher taxes
Foreigners who buy real estate in the UK will be taxed higher, this additional amount will be used to help the homeless according to Tory’s new policies
This change will neutralize the success of Corbyn’s efforts to attract young voters
The tax increase will apply to people who do not pay taxes in the UK
The finance ministry expects this policy to increase to £20 million a year - to help the homeless
Expatriates who buy real estate in the UK will be charged higher taxes - this additional money will be used to help the homeless, Prime Minister Theresa May will announce today.
This change will neutralize the success of Jeremy Corbyn’s efforts to attract young voters with a commitment to providing cheaper housing and targeting high-income audiences.
The tax increase will apply to individuals and businesses who do not pay taxes in the U.K, which will boost the government’s efforts to combat current stagnation.
Surcharge - in addition to the current tax rate, which includes higher tax rates that were announced two years ago for a second home and homes purchased only to that - can be up to three per cent.
The finance ministry expects this change will help increase to £120 million a year.
Estimates of 13 per cent of London-built properties have been sold to non-British nationals, pushing up house prices and making it more difficult for first-time homebuyers.
Many affluent areas of the country - especially in the capital - have become "ghost towns" because the majority of expatriates buy homes spend most of their time in another country.
The new policy comes just a few weeks after Boris Johnson called for a tax cut to help many young people have the opportunity to own their first home.
He accused large construction companies of keeping real estate prices so high by getting land but not in use, he called on Prime Minister May to give up quotas on affordable homes to rectify "resistance problems" in the UK.
Mr Corbyn also announced a slew of housing reforms aimed at attracting voters, including taking control of rents and ending "no-faulty" expulsions.
He would also want to give the council more power to build new homes.
Ms May said: "Yesterday I said that I was going to dedicate my tenure to revive the British dream - that life will be better for the new generation.
And that’s how to re-register the housing market that’s very turbulent today.
Britain will always be open to people who want to live, work and build their lives.
However, this doesn’t mean people who don’t live in the U.K. and overseas-based companies can easily buy a house like hard-to-working British residents.
For a lot of people, the dream of owning a house has become too far-fetched and stagnation is still huge.”
Jack Ross: 'My last ambition is to lead the Scottish team'
Sunderland's boss Jack Ross said that his "last ambition" was to become the manager of the Scotland team at some point.
Scot, a 42-year-old, is facing the challenge of resuscitating the North-Feast club, which is currently in third place in League One, is three points behind the first place.
He moved to the Light Stadium this summer after leading St Mirren back to the Scottish Premier League last season.
“I’d like to play for my country as a player.
“I had a hat with the B logo and that’s exactly what I’d like,” Ross told Sportsound of BBC Scotland.
“The years of my childhood were associated with those matches of Scotland taking place in Hampden with my father, and it’s always something I’m remembering.
However, that opportunity will only come if I succeed in managing the club.”
Sunderland's preceded managers to Ross included Dick Advocaat, David Moyes, Sam Allardyce, Martin O'Neill, Roy Keane, Gus Poyet and Paulo Di Canio.
The former Alloa Athletic manager said he didn’t feel the pressure to be his successor after the great names at such a big club he had previously turned down an offer from Barnsley and Ipswich Town.
"Succeed for me at this time will be judged by 'can I take the club back to the Premier League?'
The organizational structure and facilities at this club certainly belong to the Premier League,” he said.
"This isn't a task to achieve easily, but I only consider myself to be successful if I can bring the club back to the tournament."
Ross has only three years as a manager, after a period of assistant manager at Dumbarton and a 15-month-old as a trainer at the Hearts.
He then helped Alloa recover his position after relegation to the third place, and put St Mirren from the risk of relegation becoming the champion of the next season.
Ross said he now felt very comfortable during his playing career at Clyde, Hartlepool, Falkirk, St Mirren and Hamilton Academical.
"It's probably a real turning point," he recalls in his role leading Alloa.
“I really believe that I’m better suited as a manager than a player.
This may sound strange because I was quite successful, had a good income and achieved certain achievements when I was a player.
But the game is usually not easy.
There’s a lot you face every week.
I still have to endure the stress and pressure of the job but I am satisfied with the management.
I've always wanted to do management and now I'm doing it, I'm most comfortable to be self-sufficiency throughout my life."
You can listen to full interview on Sportsound on Sunday, August 30, on Scottish Radio from 12:00 to 13:00 BST
