A company specializes in milk pumps that divide public opinion on the internet with an advertisement that compares breast-feeding mothers with milked cows.
In order to mark the launch of the product that is considered a "wearable and unscathed milk pump in the world", consumer technology company Elvie has launched an advertisement inspired by satirical music videos to express the freedom that new suction machines bring to mothers.
The four mothers who danced in a barn full of hay follow a song with the following lyrics: “Yes, I milked myself, but you don’t see a tail and “In case you don’t realize, this isn’t the gourd, it’s my breasts.”
“Smoking milk, sucking milk out, I’m feeding the children, sucking milk out, sucking milk, I’m milking for my girls.”
However, this advertisement, posted on the company’s Facebook page, has caused a lot of controversy online.
With 77,000 views and hundreds of comments, this video has received mixed reactions from the audience, many argue that the ad underestimates the “horrorness” of the dairy industry.
"It's a bad decision to use cows to advertise this product.
Just like us, they also have to get pregnant and have children to produce milk, with the exception of calves who are taken away from the mother cow within a few days of giving birth,” a person wrote.
The Elvie breast-supplement pump is located discreetly inside the lacivatory bra (Elvie/Mother)
Another commented: "This can cause psychological damage to both mother and baby.
That’s right, why don’t you use it to advertise a breast-feeding mother?”
Another commented further: "This advertisement has nothing to do with it."
