Donggala is a capital of more than 300 km (180 miles) along the north-west coast of Sulawesi Island.
The capital, an administrative area of the province, had an estimated population of 299.200 in 2017.
Fishing and farming are the two key branches of the economy in Sulawesi prefecture, especially the Donggala coastal region.
Nickel mining is also an important industry here, but is mainly concentrated in Morowali, on the coast opposite Sulawesi.
Palu and Donggala have been attacked several times in the last 100 years, according to Indonesia's Disaster Mitigation Agency.
In 1938, a tsunami killed more more than 200 people and destroyed hundreds of houses in Donggala.
A tsunami also made landfall in western Donggala in 1996, which killed nine people.
Indonesia is located in the Pacific Ring of Fire and is often hit by earthquakes.
Here are some major earthquakes and tsunamis that have occurred in recent years:
2004: A major earthquake on the west coast of Indonesia's Aceh province in northern Sumatra on December 26 caused a tsunami to attack 14 countries, killing 226,000 people along the Indian Ocean coast, more than half of them in Aceh province.
2005: A series of powerful earthquakes struck the west coast of Sumatra at the end of March and the beginning of April.
There are hundreds of people dying in Nias, off the coast of Sumatra.
2006: An 6.8-thousand-thousand-thousand-thousand-a-half-eaquatic quake occurred south of Java, the most densely populous island in Indonesia, triggering a tsunami on the southern coast, killing nearly 700 people.
2009: An 7.6-magnitude qualter qualifier that occurred near the city of Padang, the capital in the east of Sumatra.
More than 1,100 people were killed.
A 7.5-magnitude Earthquake occurred in one of the islands of Mentawai, off the coast of Sumatra, which triggered a tsunami up to 10 meters high, destroyed dozens of villages and killed about 300 people.
2016: A light quake attacked Pidie Jaya in Aceh, causing damage and panic when people recalled the devastation of earthquakes and deadly tsunamis in 2004.
Earthquake did not cause a tsunami, but more than 100 people have been killed due to collapsed buildings.
