The U.S.-China trade war could be a good signal for Beijing
Sustaining experts said the ends of the trade war between the U.S. and China has stalled, and that the war will continue, the rift between the countries could be an advantage for Beijing.
Earlier this year, U.S. President Donald Trump, issued its first warning by slapping China’s main exports including solar panels, steel and aluminum.
Escalation has been increasing in recent weeks with a tax rate worth $200 billion (£150 billion) hitting half of Chinese goods imported into the U.S.
Beijing has retaliated by raising tariffs from five to ten percent to $60 billion of U.S. goods.
China’s pleasure of retaliating corresponds to the U.S. side’s actions and the world’s second-largest economy is unlikely to be wobbned early.
Arguing Washington means Beijing’s need to make demands, but publicly succumbing to the U.S. is too embarrassing for China’s president.
However, experts argue that if Beijing is able to come up with the right moves, the long-term pressure of the trade war with the United States can be a positive signal for China by plingizing the interdependence of the two economies.
"The fact is that a political decision in Washington or in Beijing can all start an economic relationship in either country and this is really much more dangerous than what was previously acknowledged," Abigail Grace, an Asia-centered research expert at the New American Center for Security.
