Red T-Teper Rural Rhetosis: Decreased Red Tidal Density in Pinellas, Manatee and Sarasota
The latest report from the Florida Fisheries and Wildlife Association found that the densities of the Red Tides had fallen in much of the Tampa Bay area.
According to the FWC, algae-infused phenomenon has been reported in areas of Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, and Collier - this causes the red tidal density to decrease.
Red T-thirts spread for about 130 miles along the northern coast of Pinellas to the south of Lee County.
Small plaques were also found within 10 miles off Hillsborough County, but had fewer locations than last week.
It has also been observed that the Red Tide is also observed at Pasco County.
The median density along the coast or offshore Pinellas has been reported over the past week, low-to-to-high density of Hillsborough County, low-to-high density in Manatee County, low-to-high depletion of Sarasota County, low-to-high density Charlotte County, low-to-high density along the coast or off-shore in Lee County, and low-density at Collier County.
Respiratory irritants continues to be reported in Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Lee and Collier.
Respiratory irritants have not been reported in north-West Florida this past week.
